  "problem": "The volumes of soda in cans filled in at a particular factory are normally distributed with a mean of $12\\,\\text{oz}$ and a standard deviation of $0.4\\,\\text{oz}.$ Suppose $100$ cans are randomly selected. What is the probability that the mean volume for this sample will be more than $12.1\\,\\text{oz}?$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9938\\,$ | $\\,0.9940\\,$ | $\\,0.9941\\,$ | $\\,0.9943\\,$ | $\\,0.9945\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9953\\,$ | $\\,0.9955\\,$ | $\\,0.9956\\,$ | $\\,0.9957\\,$ | $\\,0.9959\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9965\\,$ | $\\,0.9966\\,$ | $\\,0.9967\\,$ | $\\,0.9968\\,$ | $\\,0.9969\\,$ |",
